<<<
coxy. See cocky.
===
**coxy.** See ^^COCKY^^.
>>>

<<<
coyote. Pronounce koy6’ti.
===
**coyote.** Pronounce koyō'tĭ.
>>>

<<<
coze, cozy. See COSE.
===
**coze, cozy.** See ^^COSE^^.
>>>

<<<
crabbed. See -ER & -EST, 4.
===
**crabbed.** See ^^-ER^^ & ^^-EST^^, 4.
>>>

<<<
cramp, as an adjective meaning crabbed or hard to understand (c. words, terms, style), narrow (a c. corner), niggling (writes a c. hand), has now had its senses divided between crabbed & cramped, & the use of it is an affectation.
===
**cramp**, as an adjective meaning crabbed or hard to understand (*c. words, terms, style*), narrow (*a c. corner*), niggling (*writes a c. hand*), has now had its senses divided between *crabbed* & *cramped*, & the use of it is an affectation.
>>>

<<<
cran(e)age.

Spell without -e-; see MUTE E.
===
**cran(e)age.** Spell without *-e-*; see ^^MUTE E^^.
>>>

<<<
cranesbill, crane’s-bill. The apostrophe & hyphen are better dispensed with in established words of this type; cf. COCKSCOMB.
===
**cranesbill, crane's-bill.** The apostrophe & hyphen are better dispensed with in established words of this type; cf. ^^COCKSCOMB^^.
>>>

<<<
cranium. PI. -ia, -ms; see -uM.
===
**cranium.** Pl. *-ia, -ms*; see ^^-UM^^.
>>>

<<<
crape, crépe. The first is the English word, the second shop French.
===
**crape, crêpe.** The first is the English word, the second shop French.
>>>

<<<
crasis. See TECHNICAL TERMS. Pl. crases (kra’séz).
===
**crasis.** See ^^TECHNICAL TERMS^^. Pl. *crases* (krā'sēz).
>>>

<<<
crayfish, craw-. The first is the British form, the second * now used chiefly in U.S.’ (OED).
===
**crayfish, craw-.** The first is the British form, the second 'now used chiefly in U.S.' (OED).
>>>

<<<
crayon. Pronounce kra’on, & not as French.
===
**crayon.** Pronounce krā'on, & not as French.
>>>

<<<
create. See -ATABLE.
===
**create.** See ^^-ATABLE^^.
>>>

<<<
crèche. See FRENCH WORDS.
===
**crèche.** See ^^FRENCH WORDS^^.
>>>

<<<
credence, credit. Apart from the isolated phrase letter of credence & the concrete ecclesiastical sense table or shelf, credence has only one meaning—belief or trustful acceptance;
===
**credence, credit.** Apart from the isolated phrase *letter of credence* & the concrete ecclesiastical sense *table* or *shelf*, *credence* has only one meaning—belief or trustful acceptance;
>>>

<<<
the use scen in Two results stand out clearly from this investigation .. .; neither of these gives any credence to the assertions of Lord Ridley that Protectionist countries had fared better than Great Britain is a mere blunder;
===
the use seen in *Two results stand out clearly from this investigation . . .; neither of these gives any credence to the assertions of Lord Ridley that Protectionist countries had fared better than Great Britain* is a mere blunder;
>>>

<<<
give credence to means believe, simply; support or credibility is the word wanted. Credit, on the other hand, is rich in meanings,
===
*give credence to* means believe, simply; *support* or *credibility* is the word wanted. *Credit*, on the other hand, is rich in meanings,
>>>

<<<
& it is a pity that it should be allowed to deprive credence of its ewe-lamb; credence would be better in Charges like these may seem to deserve some degree of credit, & in To give entire credit to whatever he shall state.
===
& it is a pity that it should be allowed to deprive *credence* of its ewe-lamb; *credence* would be better in *Charges like these may seem to deserve some degree of credit*, & in *To give entire credit to whatever he shall state.*
>>>

<<<
Even give credit (to) has senses of its own (I give him credit for knowing better than that; No credit given on small orders), which are all the better
===
Even *give credit (to)* has senses of its own (*I give him credit for knowing better than that; No credit given on small orders*), which are all the better
>>>

<<<
+ for not being confused with the only sense of give credence to (One can give no credence to his word).
===
+ for not being confused with the only sense of *give credence to* (*One can give no credence to his word*).
>>>

<<<
credo. Pl. -os; see -0(E)s 6.
===
**credo.** Pl. *-os*; see ^^-O(E)S^^ 6.
>>>

